window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"10-10","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"10-10-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-10-01/","title_he":"א – ברכה אחרונה","title_en":"The After-Blessing","body_en":"<p>Just as the Sages instituted that a person recite a blessing before he eats, so they instituted that he recite a blessing after he eats. However, there is a difference between the first blessing and the after-blessing. The first blessing is meant to thank God for the enjoyment a person has from the food, and the Sages said (Berakhot 35a) that since it is a clear logical principle that it is forbidden for a person to enjoy anything in the world of the Holy One, blessed be He, without a blessing, there was no need for the Torah to command us about it.</p><p>But after eating, from logic alone we would not have known that one must recite a blessing, because after we have thanked God with the blessing before eating, seemingly there is no further need to bless again after eating. Therefore the Torah needed to command us specifically that after we have eaten and are satisfied, we should bless God with a comprehensive blessing, not only for the food we ate but for all the good that God has given Israel, as it is stated (Devarim 8:10): \"You shall eat and be satisfied, and you shall bless the Lord your God for the good land that He has given you.\" As we learned at length regarding Birkat Ha-mazon (4:1-4), after a person has eaten and is satisfied, he is liable to become arrogant and to be swept after desire and materialism. On the other hand, through the good taste of the foods and through the vitality he draws from them, he can rise to a broad view of all the good that God has given Israel. Therefore in Birkat Ha-mazon we do not thank God for the food alone; rather, from it we rise to a comprehensive blessing and thanksgiving for the good land and for everything connected with it – the covenant, the Torah, Jerusalem, the kingship of David and the Holy Temple. Through this, the food we ate is also elevated and uplifted, and vitality is added to it for the good. One who does not recite a blessing after eating does not elevate the spiritual side of the food and is not uplifted by it, and consequently the food in his innards becomes coarse and pulls him downward (see above 1:5).</p><p>As an extension of the Torah commandment, the Sages instituted that even one who is not satisfied, if he ate the volume of a kezayit, should recite an after-blessing. Over bread he recites Birkat Ha-mazon; over the seven species for which the Land of Israel was praised he recites Me'ein Shalosh; and over other foods he recites Borei Nefashot.</p><p>Why did they institute a blessing over the amount of a kezayit? Because it has a certain importance and benefit. The first blessing is recited over any amount, because it is over the very enjoyment of eating, and whoever enjoys even a little must recite a blessing. But the after-blessing is more comprehensive, and only if some impression of satisfaction from the eating remains can one, from it, contemplate the good that God has bestowed upon the world and recite a blessing. Therefore one who ate less than the measure of a kezayit or drank less than the measure of a revi'it does not recite a blessing (these measures will be explained below, halakhot 5-10).</p><p>Since the first blessing is over the enjoyment of eating, it must be attached to the beginning of the eating, and it is forbidden to interrupt between the blessing and the eating (above 3:1). But the after-blessing is not over the enjoyment of eating alone, but over the benefit and satisfaction a person has from what he ate; therefore it can be recited even some time later, as long as one feels some satiety from the eating (below, halakha 12).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-10-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-10-02/","title_he":"ב – ברכת מעין שלוש","title_en":"The Me'ein Shalosh Blessing","body_en":"<p>Over the seven species one recites the Me'ein Shalosh blessing, which is called 'Me'ein Shalosh' (\"an abridgment of three\") because it contains the essence of the three blessings of Birkat Ha-mazon. Corresponding to the blessing of \"Ha-zan\" (\"who feeds\"), one mentions the food that was eaten. Corresponding to the blessing over the Land, one mentions its praise, that it is \"a desirable, good and spacious land\". Corresponding to the blessing over Jerusalem, one mentions Jerusalem and the Temple. And since after the destruction the Sages added a fourth blessing – Ha-tov Ve-hametiv – they instituted saying corresponding to it as well: \"for You are good and do good to all\"; yet its name is still 'Me'ein Shalosh', corresponding to the three blessings that must be said by Torah law (Berakhot 44a; MB 208:50; above 4:1-2). The seven species are: wheat, barley, grapevine, fig, pomegranate, olive and date.</p><p>Included under wheat and barley are three more species of the grain family: spelt, oats and rye, which together with wheat and barley are the five species of grain. If one made bread from them, one recites Birkat Ha-mazon over it (as explained above 4:1), and if one made from them a cooked dish or a Mezonot baked good, their blessing is like that of the rest of the seven species – Me'ein Shalosh.</p><p>The Rishonim disagree about the status of the Me'ein Shalosh blessing. Some say its mitzva is from the Torah, because two verses before the verse in which the Torah commands us to recite Birkat Ha-mazon, the seven species for which the Land of Israel was praised are mentioned, and the implication is that after eating them too one must bless God. As it is stated (Devarim 8:8-10): \"A land of wheat and barley, and vines and figs and pomegranates, a land of olive oil and honey; a land where you will eat bread without scarcity, where you will lack nothing; a land whose stones are iron and from whose hills you will mine copper. You shall eat and be satisfied, and you shall bless the Lord your God for the good land that He has given you.\" But since there is a difference between eating bread, which is a person's main food, and eating fruits of the seven species, they instituted after eating bread to thank and praise God in a detailed formulation of three blessings, whereas after eating fruits of the seven species they instituted a single blessing, Me'ein Shalosh (Behag, Rosh and Rashba). Others say that by Torah law one is obligated to recite a blessing only after eating bread, since only bread is mentioned in the verse adjacent to the commandment. As an extension of this, the Sages instituted reciting Me'ein Shalosh after eating the seven species, for since the Land of Israel was praised for them, it is fitting, upon eating them, to say an important blessing (Rambam and Semag).<sup>1</sup></p><p>The Me'ein Shalosh blessing is special: since it was instituted for the species for which the Land of Israel was praised, the Sages instituted that the kind of food be specified explicitly. Over baked goods or cooked dishes made from species of grain, one says: \"for the sustenance and for the nourishment\" (al ha-miḥya ve-al ha-kalkala). Over fruits of the seven species, which are olive, date, grape, fig and pomegranate, one says: \"for the tree and for the fruit of the tree\" (al ha-etz ve-al pri ha-etz). And over wine one says: \"for the vine and for the fruit of the vine\" (al ha-gefen ve-al pri ha-gefen).</p><p>On Shabbat, Yom Tov and Rosh Ḥodesh, one mentions the special day in the blessing, for a person's eating on a weekday is not like his eating on a sanctified day. If he forgot and did not mention it, he has fulfilled his obligation. On Ḥanukka and Purim, which are festivals instituted by the Sages, one does not mention the day (SA 208:12).</p><p>Since the foundation of the mitzva to thank God for the food is connected with the praise of the Land of Israel, if the fruit grew in the Land of Israel, one is careful to note this in the concluding formula of the blessing: \"for the land and for its fruits\" (al ha-aretz ve-al peiroteha). And if the fruit grew outside the Land, one concludes \"for the land and for the fruits\" (al ha-aretz ve-al ha-peirot). Likewise regarding wine: if its grapes grew in the Land, one concludes \"and for the fruit of its vine\" (ve-al pri gafnah); outside the Land: \"and for the fruit of the vine\" (ve-al pri ha-gefen). When there is a doubt whether the fruits and grapes grew in the Land, one says the general formulation used outside the Land (Berakhot 44a; MB 208:54).<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"See above 4:6, note 4, that according to most Rishonim and Aḥaronim, by Torah law only one who is satisfied is obligated in Birkat Ha-mazon, as it is stated: \"and be satisfied\", and it was the Sages who instituted reciting Birkat Ha-mazon over a small amount of eating – according to R. Yehuda over a kebeitza, and according to R. Meir over a kezayit (Berakhot 49b). So it is ruled in SA 184:6. Some say that even for a kebeitza or a kezayit one is obligated by Torah law; see there. It seems that according to those who hold that the blessing over the seven species is from the Torah, even if one is not satisfied he recites an after-blessing, as Rashi wrote (39a): \"And regarding the blessing over the fruits of the Land, 'eating' is written, and eating is with a kezayit.\" So it is implied by the Sefer Ha-ḥinukh 430. And this is reasonable, since in general the fruits of the seven species are not satiating (except for Mezonot, and in a certain respect also wine and dates, as cited in SA 208:17). <b>In practice</b>, it is implied from SA 209:3 that Me'ein Shalosh is rabbinic, and consequently if one is in doubt whether he recited it, he does not recite it. But MB 209:10 wrote in the name of Aḥaronim that since some say it is from the Torah, if one was satisfied from fruits of the seven species and was in doubt whether he recited the after-blessing, it is proper that he eat another kezayit of the same species and recite a blessing before and after it, and thereby get out of the doubt. According to what I have written, it would be proper to do so even if he was not satisfied."},{"n":2,"en":"Regarding grain, according to the custom of those of Ashkenazic descent, one always says \"al ha-miḥya\", whether in the Land or outside it. According to the custom of those of Sephardic descent, in the Land one concludes \"al miḥyatah\" (\"for its sustenance\") and outside the Land \"al ha-miḥya\". Nowadays most grain is imported from abroad, and also when there is a doubt one says \"al ha-miḥya\".<br>In the blessing over Mezonot, according to the Tur and the MA, in the concluding formula too one should say: \"for the sustenance and for the nourishment\". But the Beit Yosef wrote that the Behag, the Rambam and the Semag wrote only \"for the sustenance\". Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 208:52 wrote that this is the proper practice, for this is the view of many Rishonim and Aḥaronim.<br>In the Tur 208:10: according to the Behag one says \"and we will eat of its fruit and be satisfied with its goodness\", whereas according to the Semag one does not say it, \"for one should not desire the Land for the sake of its fruit and its goodness, but rather to fulfill the mitzvot dependent on it\", and so the Rosh practiced. The Baḥ ruled that one does say it, and explained that we are asking that holiness be revealed in the fruits of the Land, \"for by eating its fruits we are nourished from the holiness of the Shekhina and from its purity, and we are satisfied with its goodness\"."}]},{"slug":"10-10-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-10-03/","title_he":"ג – מדיני ברכה 'מעין שלוש'","title_en":"Laws of the Me'ein Shalosh Blessing","body_en":"<p>It is proper to be careful to recite the Me'ein Shalosh blessing in the place where one ate and while seated, for some hold that the laws of the Me'ein Shalosh blessing are like the laws of Birkat Ha-mazon (SA 183:10; 184:3; and see above 4:13-14).</p><p>If one ate Mezonot, drank wine and ate fruits of the seven species, he mentions all three in the blessing according to the order of their importance: Mezonot, wine and fruits (SA 208:12). If he forgot to mention one of them, sometimes what he mentioned includes what he forgot and exempts it <i>be-di'avad</i>, and sometimes it does not include it, and he must recite the blessing again.<sup>3</sup></p><p>If one ate Mezonot in an amount that obligates an after-blessing, and a fruit of the seven species or wine in an amount about which there is a doubt whether he became obligated in an after-blessing – since in any case he must recite Me'ein Shalosh for the Mezonot, it is good that he also mention the fruits and the wine in the blessing in order to get out of the doubt (Taz 208:19).</p><p>If one ate Mezonot, and his friend ate Mezonot and fruits of the seven species, if the one who ate Mezonot does not remember the blessing well, he may fulfill his obligation by hearing the blessing from his friend, even though his friend also mentions the fruits, for the mention of the fruits is not considered an interruption for him (Birkei Yosef 213:1).</p><p>One who eats a kezayit of a fruit of the seven species and additional tree fruits that are not of the seven species recites Me'ein Shalosh and exempts the other fruits as well, for since he gives thanks for the fruits in the Me'ein Shalosh blessing, he exempts all the tree fruits he ate. But if he ate a fruit of the seven species and a fruit of the ground, he recites Me'ein Shalosh over the tree fruit and Borei Nefashot over the fruit of the ground.<sup>4</sup></p><p>If one drank wine and other beverages – just as before drinking, the Ha-gafen blessing exempts the other beverages (above 7:4), so after drinking, the Me'ein Shalosh blessing over the wine exempts the other beverages (SA 208:15).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"If in the concluding formula he mentioned only \"al ha-miḥya\", he has <i>be-di'avad</i> also exempted wine and dates (for they too are considered nourishing and somewhat sustaining), but not other fruits (Birkei Yosef 208:7, 14). Therefore if, for example, he also ate grapes, he must recite the blessing again for them. If he mentioned \"al ha-gefen\", he has <i>be-di'avad</i> also exempted grapes, but not Mezonot and fruits (SA 208:15). If he mentioned \"al ha-etz\", he has <i>be-di'avad</i> also exempted wine, but not Mezonot (BHL 208:18; Tzitz Eliezer 19:8:2)."},{"n":4,"en":"So wrote SA 208:13. Some say that the blessing over fruits exempts fruits of the ground as well, and so wrote the Rashbatz and the Ḥayei Adam. Halikhot Olam (vol. 2, p. 108) took their view into account. But the prevalent custom is like the SA, that one must recite Borei Nefashot over fruits of the ground, and so wrote Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 64 and Kaf Ha-ḥayim 73. And although as a rule one gives Me'ein Shalosh precedence over Borei Nefashot, since it is a more important blessing, when there is an opinion that the Me'ein Shalosh blessing exempts the food over which one must recite Borei Nefashot, and the halakha was ruled that it does not exempt it, as in our case, it is preferable to recite Borei Nefashot first (MB 208:64).<br>If one erred and recited \"al ha-miḥya\" over rice, he has fulfilled his obligation. If he ate Mezonot and rice, the poskim disagree whether the \"al ha-miḥya\" blessing will exempt the rice. <i>Le-khatḥila</i> it is preferable to eat something else whose blessing is Borei Nefashot, and if he did not eat such, he should exempt the rice as well with the \"al ha-miḥya\" blessing (Ben Ish Ḥai, Pinḥas 18)."}]},{"slug":"10-10-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-10-04/","title_he":"ד – בורא נפשות","title_en":"Borei Nefashot","body_en":"<p>One who eats the measure of a kezayit of other foods that are not of the seven species, or drinks a revi'it of other beverages that are not wine, recites Borei Nefashot. Originally the Borei Nefashot blessing was optional, and the great Amora Rav would recite it only after eating important foods such as meat and eggs. Over time the custom was accepted as an obligation to recite Borei Nefashot after all eating or drinking (see Berakhot 44b).</p><p>Since the first blessing is over the enjoyment of the eating itself, the Sages instituted a special blessing for each kind of food, so that we thank God for every food in a detailed manner. But the blessing after eating is over the general benefit a person derives from the eating, and therefore they instituted one blessing for several kinds. For example, if he ate rice, a tree fruit, a fruit of the ground and meat, and drank juice, he recites four blessings at the outset – Mezonot over the rice, Ha-etz over the tree fruit, Ha-adama over the fruit of the ground, and Shehakol over the meat and the juice. But after eating them he recites Borei Nefashot over all of them, for this blessing exempts all kinds of foods and beverages except for the seven species (SA 207:1).</p><p>In the Borei Nefashot blessing we thank and praise God for having created many living beings (nefashot), and for having created all the foods that are essential to supply their lacks so that they can survive; and He further created additional foods so that they might delight in them and with them sustain the soul of every living being. For the delight in the additional foods also adds vitality to a person and enriches his mind and his emotions.</p><p>Borei Nefashot is unlike Shehakol. Shehakol is a general blessing and <i>be-di'avad</i> exempts all foods; therefore one who does not know which blessing to recite over a certain food – Mezonot or Shehakol, for example – may recite Shehakol over it. By contrast, with the after-blessing, one blessing does not exempt another: if he is obligated to recite Me'ein Shalosh, he cannot recite Borei Nefashot in its place, and likewise the reverse (MB 202:55).<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"However, according to the Knesset Ha-gedola, Mahari\"tz, Ginat Veradim, Kaf Ha-ḥayim 202:79, Eretz Tzvi 1:29 and Iggerot Moshe OḤ 1:74, <i>be-di'avad</i> Borei Nefashot exempts Me'ein Shalosh, just as Shehakol exempts everything. Therefore in a case of doubt one may recite Borei Nefashot. Likewise, when one does not remember the text of Me'ein Shalosh, he may recite Borei Nefashot. But according to most poskim, Borei Nefashot does not exempt Me'ein Shalosh, as emerges from SA 202:11, and so wrote MA 208:26, MB 202:55, BHL s.v. \"u-vorei nefashot\", and Birkat Hashem vol. 2, 2, notes 6-8; 2:7-8. Therefore, in a case of doubt or when one does not know how to recite Me'ein Shalosh, one should not recite any blessing at all. However, if he recited Borei Nefashot, he should not then recite Me'ein Shalosh, out of concern for those who hold that he already fulfilled his obligation with the Borei Nefashot blessing (Yabia Omer vol. 1, 12:7). See below note 14 on whether one who eats half a measure of one of the seven species and half a measure of another kind is obligated in an after-blessing."}]},{"slug":"10-10-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-10-05/","title_he":"ה – שיעור כזית לברכה אחרונה","title_en":"The Measure of a Kezayit for the After-Blessing","body_en":"<p>One who eats even a minute amount, since he enjoyed it, is obligated to recite a blessing over it at the outset. But an after-blessing is recited only by one who ate at least the measure of an olive (kezayit), for the Sages said: \"There is no eating less than a kezayit.\" That is, eating less than a kezayit is so little that no impression of satisfaction remains from it over which one could recite a blessing.</p><p>As a result of the exiles, a doubt arose about the volume of an olive. Some say that the olive found among us is smaller than the olive the Sages spoke of, which is a little less than a third of an egg. And some say it is about half an egg. And although it appears from many Rishonim that the olive the Sages spoke of is the olive found among us, since we hold the rule \"in cases of doubt regarding blessings we are lenient\", it was ruled in halakha that one should not recite an after-blessing over less than the volume of half of an average egg of our day. And although it is reasonable that the volume of the olive has not changed, one can explain that since the Jewish people have been accustomed to regard the measure of a kezayit as the volume of half an egg, nowadays, according to our perception, one who eats less than the measure of half an egg is considered to have eaten an amount that has no significance for reciting a blessing over it. And since the basis for setting the measure of a kezayit as obligating an after-blessing is rabbinic, the custom of the Jewish people has the force of a measure set by the Sages.</p><p>One need not be anxious about estimating the volume of half an egg, for when people are given something to estimate, without doubt some will err upward and some will err downward, and nevertheless the Sages entrusted these measures to each and every person. In addition, we have learned that according to most Rishonim the measure of an olive is much smaller than half an egg, and already over that amount it would have been proper to recite a blessing according to the essential enactment of the Sages. Therefore, whenever it seems to a person that he ate the volume of half an egg, it is a mitzva for him to recite a blessing.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"The Sages said: \"There is no eating less than a kezayit\" (Torat Kohanim 12:2, and other places). This is the law both regarding punishment for eating forbidden foods and regarding a mitzva, such as eating matza. Likewise we learned in Berakhot 49b that eating is called eating with a kezayit, and eating that has some satiety in it is the measure of a kebeitza (egg's volume). And there, 39a, it is explained that the measure for the after-blessing instituted by the Sages is over food of the volume of an average olive.<br>From many Ge'onim and Rishonim it is implied that a kezayit is like the common olive, and therefore they did not calculate its volume, because the common olive is itself the measure. The significance of this measure is that it is no longer a mere crumb but has some significance of eating food. In practice, the volume of the common olive in the Land of Israel is up to 7.5 cc. It is likewise attested in the name of R. Ḥayim of Volozhin and the Avnei Nezer that they practiced this way in practice regarding eating matza. MA 486:1 learned from the words of the Rambam that the measure of an olive is a little less than a third of an egg, and this can be learned from other Rishonim as well. From the Rashba it emerges that an olive is a little less than a quarter of an egg. Ri in Tosafot proved from several Gemaras that an olive is about half an egg. So wrote several other Ashkenazic Rishonim (Agudda, Mahari\"v and Maharil). Some hold that in the places of the great Ashkenazic sages olives were not common, and therefore they decided according to what they inferred from the Gemara, and had they seen the olives common in the Land of Israel they would have retracted. <b>However, in practice</b>, although the view of the Tosafists is the view of a minority of the Rishonim, because of the doubt SA 486:1 wrote, regarding the mitzva of eating matza, that a kezayit is half an egg. And the same is the law regarding the after-blessing (MB 486:1). MA 486:1 wrote that it means a little less than half an egg. In practice, R. Ḥayim Na'eh estimated that an egg is about 57 cc, and half an egg without its shell about 27 cc. According to MB one calculates with the shell, and that comes to about 29 cc. Since according to many the shell is not calculated, and according to MA it means a little less than half an egg, the practice is to calculate about 27 cc. However, according to precise calculations based on the measure of the dirham, it emerges that an egg is about 50 cc, and half of it with its shell about 25 cc, and without its shell 22.5 cc (see Middot Ve-shiurei Torah by Rav Benish 15-16, and the article 'Shiur Kezayit', pp. 521-532; and see below note 11).<br>Some later Ashkenazic authorities held that the eggs of our day have shrunk by about half, and in their view the measure of half an egg is like the measure of a whole egg – about 50 cc (for elaboration see below note 11). The custom of those of Sephardic descent is not to take this view into account at all in any matter. Even the Ḥazon Ish, who strengthened this view to the point that it is called by his name, agreed that regarding a kezayit this is a stringency, for the measure for the after-blessing is the kezayit common in that generation, as distinct from an egg, which is a precise measure according to which mikva'ot and all volume measures are calculated (Middot Ve-shiurei Torah p. 36, note 32). It should be added that in the last generation poultry farming has improved, and the volume of eggs has grown by about twenty percent; consequently, when one estimates a kezayit according to what we know as half an egg, there is no need to worry that one did not eat the amount that obligates an after-blessing."}]},{"slug":"10-10-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-10-06/","title_he":"ו – נפח ולא משקל","title_en":"Volume, Not Weight","body_en":"<p>The measures the Sages spoke of are measures of volume, not weight. For it is clear that Jews did not walk around with a scale in their pocket, and therefore the Sages gave their measures according to common things, such as an olive and an egg, which can be estimated by sight. As we have learned, the practical ruling is that the measure of an olive is about half an egg. If the food has large hollows, they are not counted toward the volume, but small holes that are not very noticeable do not change the calculation, and whenever it appears that the volume of the food is about half an egg including the small holes, one recites an after-blessing over it.</p><p>There were, however, Sephardic poskim who were accustomed to measure the measures by weight, because it is difficult to estimate volume: sometimes the food is long and sometimes square, and sometimes it has large hollows that are not counted toward the volume. In addition, some hold that the small holes too should be subtracted from the measure of volume, and it is very difficult to estimate their volume; therefore they made use of weight. But they too agree that in principle the measure depends on volume and not on weight. This has been the custom of the Jewish people from time immemorial: to estimate according to volume and to include the small holes in the measure, and only the large hollows, which are not part of the texture of the food, were not included in the measure. And this is the halakha.</p><p>For example, in bread, the large hollows that sometimes form in it are not included in the measure, but the small holes that run throughout it are included in the measure of half an egg. The same law applies to rice cakes, popcorn, Bamba, whipped cream and the like: since they have no large hollows inside them, one goes according to the volume before us.</p><p>Every person should learn to estimate foods relative to half an egg. We have already learned that one need not worry about error, for the Sages gave every person the authority to estimate the measure himself, even though it is quite possible that he will err somewhat upward or downward.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"Mishna Uktzin 2:8: \"Spongy bread is measured as it is.\" Thus one goes according to volume. Even the holes in the food are included in the measure of volume, for spongy bread has holes, and nevertheless the Mishna said: \"It is measured as it is.\" Only large hollows are not included, as stated further in the Mishna: \"And if it has a hollow, one compresses its hollow.\" So wrote Ginat Veradim, Even Ha-ozer [?], MA 486:1, Arukh Ha-shulḥan 486:2, and others. However, on the other hand, Responsa Zera Emet 1:29 wrote that small holes are not included. Maḥazik Berakha 210:3 and others agreed with him. On this basis, together with the other problems connected with measuring volume, some Sephardic poskim wrote to measure by weight, since in this way one can calculate in a manner closer to the truth. So wrote Ben Ish Ḥai, Mas'ei 2. And so ruled Rav Ovadia Yosef and Rav Mordekhai Eliyahu, that the measure of a kezayit is about 29 grams. However, in principle all agree that the essential thing is volume, for otherwise the Mishna in Uktzin, \"Spongy bread is measured as it is\", would be void. Only because of the difficulty of calculating volume without hollows did they make use of weight. So wrote Rav Ovadia Yosef regarding ḥalla (Yeḥaveh Da'at 1:16; 4:55). And the view of Rav Ben-Tzion Abba Shaul (Or Le-Tziyon vol. 2, 14:17) and Rav Shalom Messas (Yalkut Shemesh 137) is that in practice one calculates according to volume and not weight, as is the view of most Sephardic poskim of previous generations (as cited in Maḥazik Berakha 486:2, and so wrote the Zivḥei Tzedek and the Pri To'ar; and see Peninei Halakha: Pesaḥ 16:23-24).<br>It seems in practice that one should always go according to the volume before us, unlike the words of the Zera Emet, for otherwise our words would be given over to measurements, whereas the Sages wanted to establish their words and not leave them in perpetual doubt. And this is the custom, for otherwise it would be impossible to instruct people to estimate a kezayit. So it appears from the words of Rav Sherira Gaon (cited in the Eshkol, Hilkhot Ḥalla): \"The measure is given into the hand of each person to estimate according to his own judgment – by eyesight.\" So too it is cited in the Responsa of the Ge'onim (Zikhron La-rishonim, Harkavy 268): \"Each one, when he acts according to his own judgment, has fulfilled his obligation.\" Only the large hollows that are not part of the texture of the food are not included in the measure. This is the view of most poskim of our time, and so it is cited in Ve-zot Ha-berakha, Clarification 1, that Rav Ovadia Yosef agreed that the small holes are included in the measure.<br>To this one should add the view that a 'zayit' is like our olive, which is how several of the great Aḥaronim practiced. One should also add the view that a 'zayit' is less than a third of an egg. And although we are stringent to estimate it as half an egg, one should not be further stringent and calculate half an egg by its weight or without the small holes. Those who wish to be stringent in everything and calculate by weight must be careful not to eat the volume of half an egg that does not have the weight of half an egg, for if they eat it, their stringency will lead them to leniency: they will have eaten something that according to all opinions obligates an after-blessing and not recited it."}]},{"slug":"10-10-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-10-07/","title_he":"ז – שיעור אכילת פרס","title_en":"The Measure of Akhilat Peras","body_en":"<p>If one ate the measure of a kezayit with long pauses, then if from the beginning of his eating to its end less than the time-measure of 'akhilat peras' (the time it takes to eat a peras) passed, his eating is still considered one act of eating, and he recites an after-blessing after it. But if he ate the measure of a kezayit over a longer time, his eating is not considered one act of eating, and he does not recite an after-blessing over it.</p><p>A 'peras' is the amount of bread people were accustomed to eat at one meal. But a doubt arose as to how many minutes it actually takes an average person to eat the 'measure of a peras'. According to those who give a longer time – nine minutes; and according to those who give a shorter time – three minutes. In practice, it is good to finish the measure of a kezayit within four minutes; however, even one who ate the measure of a kezayit (half an egg) over about seven minutes is obligated to recite a blessing. For the measure of 'akhilat peras' was set according to people in general, and since quite a few people eat the measure of a peras in about seven minutes, this measure is considered a measure of eating.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"'Peras' means half in Aramaic. In the time of Ḥazal people were accustomed to eat two meals a day, and for this purpose they would bake a loaf of bread sufficient for the two meals. It follows that the measure of a 'peras' is the length of time it took an average person to eat half of that loaf. In other words, it is the length of time in which one eats the bread that people are accustomed to eat at one meal. But the Tanna'im (Eruvin 82b) and the Rishonim disagree about its exact measure. According to Rashi it is the volume of four eggs, and according to the Rambam three eggs. In halakha it was ruled that in Torah laws one is stringent and in rabbinic laws one is lenient (see SA 409:7, 618:7; MB 612:8). Regarding blessings, since in cases of doubt regarding blessings we are lenient, one estimates with three eggs (MB 210:1). In terms of minutes: some say 9 minutes (Ḥatam Sofer 6:16); some say 8 (Gra\"z, Sdei Ḥemed); some say 7.5 (Bikkurei Ya'akov; Mo'ed Le-khol Ḥai 20:33; Arukh La-ner, Sukka 639:13 [?]; Melamed Le-ho'il OḤ 113:5; Pitḥa Zuta 639:2); some say 7 (Rav Frank in the name of Rav Yitzḥak Elḥanan of Kovno); some say 6-7 (Tzemaḥ Tzedek); some say 5 (others in the name of Rav Yitzḥak Elḥanan of Kovno); some say one should be stringent down to 4 (Rav Ḥayim Na'eh); some say down to 3-4 (Arukh Ha-shulḥan 202:8); some say down to 3 (Ḥazon Ish and Iggerot Moshe OḤ 4:41); and some say even down to 2 (Shevet Sofer). And although <i>le-khatḥila</i> it is good to finish the kezayit in four minutes, in practice it seems that whoever ate within seven minutes must recite a blessing. One should not say that the custom is 4 minutes, for it is only in the books of our generation that it became customary to write this (see Ve-zot Ha-berakha p. 42, and Piskei Teshuvot 210, notes 10-12), but this is not the actual reality, that akhilat peras is 4 minutes. One should also add the many opinions that the measure of a 'zayit' is much smaller than half an egg; therefore, if one ate the volume of half an egg in seven minutes, he recites a blessing, and so ruled Rav Yisraeli. And in Yeḥaveh Da'at 1:17, which tends to be concerned about doubtful blessings, he wrote that one may recite a blessing up to 7.5 minutes, and one may rely on him, for the measure need not be set according to the average, but must also take into account those who eat slowly, as long as they are close to the average. But I wrote seven because it is a round figure. It should be noted that according to most poskim the practice is to measure 'akhilat peras' according to eating ordinary bread; however, according to the Minḥat Ḥinukh and Torat Ḥesed OḤ 32, for each and every food one goes according to the ease or difficulty of eating it, and consequently it emerges that according to them, for most foods the measure is longer."}]},{"slug":"10-10-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-10-08/","title_he":"ח – הידורים בדין השיעורים (בריה וכזית)","title_en":"Being Scrupulous Regarding the Measures (Beriya and Kezayit)","body_en":"<p>Some say that one who eats a whole beriya (an entire natural item), such as a single grape, or a pomegranate seed, a chickpea or a pea, must recite an after-blessing. Even though it does not contain the measure of a kezayit, since it has special importance, being a whole entity, one should recite an after-blessing over it (based on the Yerushalmi). And although it was ruled in halakha that whoever did not eat the measure of a kezayit does not recite a blessing (Rif and Rambam), nevertheless <i>le-khatḥila</i>, in order not to enter into a doubt, it is proper not to eat a beriya without eating an amount that obligates an after-blessing (Rosh; SA 210:1). If he bites off a piece of the beriya and afterward eats the rest, since he did not put the whole beriya in his mouth at once, he is not considered as having eaten a beriya (Ben Ish Ḥai, Matot 5). Likewise, if he does not swallow the pit, as with the pit of a date, an olive or a cherry, he is not considered as having eaten a beriya (MB 210:7, 9).<sup>9</sup></p><p>Likewise, it is proper for those who are scrupulous to eat either less than the measure of our olive, in which case according to all opinions they will not recite an after-blessing; or the volume of half an egg or more, in which case according to all opinions they will recite an after-blessing. But if they eat from the volume of an olive up to less than the volume of half an egg, according to some poskim they will need to recite a blessing, but in practice they will not recite a blessing, for in cases of doubt regarding blessings we are lenient (above, halakha 5).</p><p>All this is for those who wish to be stringent with themselves, but according to the letter of the law there is no obligation in this, and one who wants to eat a doubtful amount may do so, and afterward he should not recite an after-blessing, in accordance with the rule that in cases of doubt regarding blessings we are lenient.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"In the Yerushalmi (Berakhot 6:1) it is explained that one who eats a beriya is obligated to recite an after-blessing. According to the Rif, the Rambam and many others, the Bavli (Berakhot 39a) disagrees with the Yerushalmi, and if so, only one who eats the measure of a kezayit recites an after-blessing. On the other hand, according to the Rosh, the Rashba and Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona, it is possible that the Bavli does not disagree with the Yerushalmi, and if so, one recites an after-blessing even over a beriya that is not the measure of a kezayit. SA 210:1 wrote that it is proper to be careful not to eat a beriya that is less than a kezayit."}]},{"slug":"10-10-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-10-09/","title_he":"ט – שיעור ברכה אחרונה בעוגה","title_en":"The Measure for the After-Blessing over Cake","body_en":"<p>One who eats <b>cake</b> in the volume of a kezayit (the volume of half an egg) recites Al Ha-miḥya. Although among the ingredients of the cake there are eggs, sugar and oil, so that the flour itself that he ate does not amount to a kezayit, since the other ingredients are subsidiary to the flour and are meant to flavor it, they combine with it to make up the measure of a kezayit. This is on condition that the flour is at least <b>one-eighth</b> of the other ingredients (SA 208:9; MB 43, 48). In practice, in almost all our cakes, including whipped [?] (batter) cakes, there is at least one-eighth flour.</p><p>If the amount of flour in the dough is less than one-eighth of all the ingredients, after eating a kezayit of the cake one does not recite Al Ha-miḥya but Borei Nefashot. For since the flour in this cake is so little, it does not draw the other ingredients after it but is drawn after them. But before eating it one must recite Mezonot, because regarding the first blessing no specific measure is required; rather, as long as the flour too is meant to give flavor, it is the main ingredient, and the blessing is determined by it (SA 208:2-3; below 11:4).</p><p><b>In a pashtida (savory pie) and a patty</b>, the other ingredients are not meant only to flavor the flour, as in a cake, but have value of their own. Therefore, only if the flour is the <b>majority</b> of the mixture does one recite Al Ha-miḥya over eating a kezayit of the pashtida. But if the flour is the <b>minority</b>, the other ingredients do not combine with it, and therefore only if he ate an amount of a kezayit (half an egg) of the flour itself within the time of akhilat peras (7 minutes) does he recite Al Ha-miḥya – for example, if he ate the measure of three olives in seven minutes from a pashtida whose flour is one-third of all its ingredients. But if he did not eat such an amount, whenever he ate an amount of a kezayit from the pashtida he recites Borei Nefashot.</p><p>A cake or pashtida that has a filling that is not mixed into the flour: since the filling stands on its own, it does not combine with the flour, and only if he ate a kezayit of the dough together with all its ingredients does he recite Al Ha-miḥya. If only together with the filling did he eat the measure of a kezayit, he recites Borei Nefashot. For example, if he ate strudel whose dough does not amount to a kezayit, but together with the apples inside it there is a kezayit, he recites Borei Nefashot. And if the dough amounted to a kezayit, he recites Al Ha-miḥya (see below, note 14).<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"On this law there are three opinions: <b>a)</b> The law of the after-blessing is like the law of the first blessing, and whatever contains any of the five species of grain, its blessing is Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya, even if it does not contain a kezayit of grain within the time of akhilat peras (so it is implied from the Rif, the Rambam, Tosafot and the Rosh; see BHL 208:9, s.v. \"ve-aḥarav\"). <b>b)</b> If the mixture contains a kezayit of grain within [the amount eaten in] the time of akhilat peras, then even if he eats only a kezayit, although he did not eat a kezayit of the grain, he recites Al Ha-miḥya (Ra'ah and Ritva). <b>c)</b> Only if he actually eats a kezayit of grain does he recite Al Ha-miḥya; therefore, if the kezayit of grain is mixed into more than the amount of akhilat peras, then even if he eats it continuously, it turns out that he did not eat a kezayit of grain within the time of akhilat peras, and therefore he does not recite Al Ha-miḥya after it (the Gra in explaining Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona and the SA; and so wrote Responsa Mikveh Ha-mayim 5:14 and other Aḥaronim).<br>In practice, the custom regarding cake follows the second opinion (based on MA, Pri Megadim, Ḥayei Adam, MB 208:48; and so wrote Benei Tziyon vol. 3, OḤ 208:9; Or Le-Tziyon vol. 2, 14:23; Ḥazon Ovadia, Berakhot p. 190). According to the Rambam, a kezayit within [the amount of] akhilat peras is less than one-ninth, and according to Tosafot and Rashi one-eighth, and so wrote MB 208:43, Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 208:45 and the Zivḥei Tzedek. This applies specifically to cake and cookies, where the other ingredients come to flavor the flour and are drawn after it. But regarding pashtidot and other cooked dishes, only if he actually ate a kezayit of grain within the time of akhilat peras does he recite Al Ha-miḥya, according to the third opinion."}]},{"slug":"10-10-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-10-10/","title_he":"י – שיעור ברכה אחרונה בשתייה","title_en":"The Measure for the After-Blessing over Drinking","body_en":"<p>One who drinks a 'revi'it ha-log', which is the volume of an egg and a half, is obligated in an after-blessing. Some say that the law of drinking is like the law of eating, and one who drinks the volume of a kezayit – half an egg – is obligated in an after-blessing (Tosafot). Those who are stringent are accustomed to take their view into account and not to drink an amount of more than half an egg and less than a revi'it. However, in practice there is no obligation to take their view into account, and whoever drank less than a revi'it, about 75 milliliters, should not recite a blessing (SA 210:1. This measure is less than half an ordinary cup. In the note it is explained that the measure is 75 ml and not 86 ml).<sup>11</sup></p><p>The duration: some say that the law of drinking is like the law of eating, and if one drank a revi'it over the time of akhilat peras (7 minutes), he is obligated to recite an after-blessing (Ra'avad). And some say that the law of drinking differs from the law of eating, for since people are accustomed to drink continuously, if one drank the measure of a revi'it more slowly than is customary for drinking a revi'it, he does not recite an after-blessing (MA, based on the Rambam). Since we hold the rule that in cases of doubt regarding blessings we are lenient, whoever drinks a revi'it beyond the time in which it is customary to drink a revi'it does not recite a blessing. But if he drank continuously, with slight pauses for sipping and breathing, he is obligated to recite an after-blessing.</p><p>According to several poskim, one who drinks a beverage that people are accustomed to drink slowly, such as hot coffee or soup, even though he did not drink it continuously, recites an after-blessing after it, because this is the manner of drinking it (Ginat Veradim). Some have practiced this way. However, according to many poskim, one should not distinguish between a hot drink and a cold one, and whoever drank the revi'it in more time than people are accustomed to drink a revi'it does not recite an after-blessing. <i>Le-khatḥila</i>, in order to satisfy all opinions, it is good that he eat or drink an additional food or beverage, so that he can recite Borei Nefashot over it without doubt.<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"The Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Berakhot 1:2) that one who drinks a revi'it is obligated in an after-blessing, and nearly all the Rishonim agreed. But Tosafot wrote in Berakhot 39a and Yoma 79a that it is possible that the measure is a kezayit, or the majority of a revi'it, or a kebeitza. SA 210:1 wrote that <i>le-khatḥila</i> it is good to take Tosafot into account (and an olive according to Tosafot is half an egg). MB 210:12 wrote, based on MA and Taz, that even if one drank a kezayit, it is still good to be careful not to drink the majority of a revi'it, and all the more so not to drink a kebeitza, because the higher he goes in the measures, the greater the concern that he becomes obligated in an after-blessing; but in practice he should not recite a blessing, for the halakha follows most poskim, who hold that only one who drinks a revi'it recites an after-blessing.<br>According to nearly all the Rishonim and Aḥaronim, the measure of an egg is the measure of an egg of our day. However, one of the great Aḥaronim in Ashkenaz, Rabbi Yeḥezkel Landau, author of the Noda Bi-Yehuda, on the basis of calculations he made (Tzlaḥ, Pesaḥim 116b; and see BHL 271:13), concluded that our eggs have become much smaller and nowadays their volume is about half the volume of the eggs in the time of Ḥazal. So wrote the Ḥatam Sofer OḤ 127. The Ḥazon Ish established this measure and calculated that the volume of an egg is about 100 cc, and a revi'it ha-log about 150 cc. In practice, the halakha follows the accepted measure, for so ruled nearly all the poskim. However, in mitzvot whose basis is from the Torah, many of those of Ashkenazic descent are accustomed <i>le-khatḥila</i> to take the view of the Noda Bi-Yehuda into account, but in rabbinic mitzvot, and certainly regarding the measure for the after-blessing, they do not take his view into account (MB 271:68; 486:1; and see Peninei Halakha: Pesaḥ 16:8; 16:23, note 20). Only the students of the Ḥazon Ish do not recite an after-blessing over less than 150 ml (and see Piskei Teshuvot 210, notes 15-16).<br>Let us return to clarifying the accepted measure that was passed down by tradition from the period of the Ge'onim. They estimated it according to the weight of water in the volume of a coin that was widespread in the lands of the East from the period of the Ge'onim until the end of Turkish rule. At first the coin was called zuz, and afterward it was called in Arabic 'dirham'. This is what the Rambam wrote, that the measure of a revi'it is close to 27 dirhams. R. Ḥayim Na'eh calculated the weight of the dirham and it came out to 3.205 grams, and according to this a revi'it is about 86 cc (the numerical value of 'kos', cup), and an egg about 57 cc. So they wrote and ruled for about fifty years. But it has become clear with certainty that his measure is not accurate, for he measured according to the 'dirham' that was accepted in his time under Turkish rule. But for hundreds of years, and in the time of the Rambam, the 'dirham' weighed only 2.83 grams; it follows that the measure of a revi'it according to what the Ge'onim and the Rambam established is 75 cc, and an egg 50 cc (Rav Benish in Middot Ve-shiurei Torah 30:5; 13:1; 16:6). In Ashkenaz they estimated an egg at about 46 cc (ibid. 13:5, based on the father of the Shela, the Taz, the Shakh and the MA). According to archaeological studies collected by Prof. Zohar Amar, it emerged that an egg in the Land of Israel was about 42-43 cc. In practice, one should follow the precise measure of the dirham, even though many are still accustomed to write according to R. Ḥayim Na'eh (see Halikhot Olam vol. 1, p. 291; Birkat Hashem vol. 2, 1, note 40). To this one should add the Ashkenazic custom of estimating an egg at 46 cc, as well as the archaeological studies and those who hold that already with a kezayit one is obligated in an after-blessing (as I wrote above in note 7, in this matter too one need not fear error; rather, the authority is given to each person to estimate by his own sight whether he drank the volume of 75 cc – all the more so since the eggs marketed in our generation are more than twenty percent larger than eggs in the past, since they come from improved breeds of hens, and the hens are fattened with rich feed)."},{"n":12,"en":"According to the Rambam, one who drinks forbidden beverages is punished if he \"drank little by little\" within the time of drinking a revi'it. According to the Ra'avad, even if he drank within the measure of akhilat peras he is punished (Shevitat He-asor 2:4; Ma'akhalot Asurot 14:9; Terumot 10:3). SA 612:10 leans toward the view of the Rambam, and the Gra held like the Ra'avad. MA 210:1 wrote that this is also the law regarding the after-blessing: according to the Rambam, if he drank over more than the time for drinking a revi'it he does not recite a blessing, and this is the view of most poskim. However, according to the Knesset Ha-gedola and the Beit Yehuda, the dispute between the Rambam and the Ra'avad is only regarding drinking something forbidden – when one receives lashes – but regarding the after-blessing, both would agree that even if one eats or drinks over more than the measure of akhilat peras, he must recite a blessing, since in practice he enjoyed it, for the beverage or the food is inside his innards. According to the Gra\"z and the Ḥatam Sofer, the words of the Rambam refer only to prohibitions, but regarding the after-blessing he would agree with the Ra'avad that if one drank within akhilat peras, he must recite a blessing. According to Ginat Veradim and Maharam Schick, the Rambam's view is indeed that if one drank an ordinary beverage over more than the time for drinking a revi'it he does not recite a blessing, but when it is a hot or strong drink or soup, which is normally drunk slowly, he recites an after-blessing if he drank a revi'it within akhilat peras. So practiced the author of Sho'el U-meshiv, the Minḥat Ḥinukh and the Arukh La-ner. Those who practice like them have what to rely on (for here there is a combination of four doubts in favor of obligating an after-blessing – a) the view of the Ra'avad, b) Knesset Ha-gedola, c) Gra\"z, d) Ginat Veradim; and see Piskei Teshuvot 210, notes 23-25).<br>However, on the other hand, most poskim were concerned even in this case about a doubtful blessing, and ruled not to recite a blessing. So wrote the Pri Ḥadash, the Ḥida, the Ḥayei Adam, the Ben Ish Ḥai and MB 210:1. (See below 12:3, note 4, that there is a dispute whether a double doubt [sfek sfeka] is combined in order to recite a blessing, and the custom is not to recite a blessing.) If he leaves a revi'it of the coffee or tea to cool and drinks it continuously, he can recite a blessing according to all opinions (and so wrote MB 210:1)."}]},{"slug":"10-10-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-10-11/","title_he":"יא – גבינה, לבן וגלידה דינם כאוכל","title_en":"Cheese, Leben and Ice Cream Are Treated as Food","body_en":"<p>We have learned that there is a significant difference between a food and a beverage: one recites an after-blessing over a food in the measure of a kezayit (half an egg) eaten within the time of akhilat peras (7 minutes), and over a beverage in the measure of a revi'it (75 ml, the volume of an egg and a half) drunk continuously in the customary manner. Two main signs distinguish between a food and a beverage. The first is that a beverage is a liquid, and if it is placed on a plate it will spread out in all directions; when it does not spread out to the sides, it is a food. The second is that a food is chewed with the teeth, or at least worked a little with the tongue, whereas a beverage is drunk.</p><p>Therefore leben (a cultured milk product), cheese, jelly, pudding and ice cream, since they are not fit for drinking and are worked a little with the tongue, are treated as food, and one recites an after-blessing over eating the measure of a kezayit (half an egg) within the time of akhilat peras (7 minutes). If the leben is shaken until it becomes liquid, its law will be like that of a beverage. And soup, even if it is thick, as long as it spreads like a liquid, its law is like that of a beverage.</p><p>An ice pop made of frozen water with sugar: one who sucks or licks it while it is outside his mouth is treated as one who drinks, and since it is not possible for him to manage to suck a revi'it within the time in which people are accustomed to drink a revi'it, he should not recite an after-blessing after it. But if he bites the ice pop, even if afterward he sucks it inside his mouth, it is treated as food, and if he ate of it the measure of half an egg within about 7 minutes, he recites an after-blessing.<sup>13</sup></p><p>If one drank the measure of a revi'it from two different kinds of beverages whose blessing is the same, or ate the measure of a kezayit from two different foods whose blessing is the same, they combine together to obligate him in an after-blessing. But if he drank half a measure of a beverage and ate half a measure of a food, he does not recite an after-blessing, for the measures of food and drink do not combine (MB 210:1).<sup>14</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":13,"en":"In SA 208:6, regarding porridge that contains flour: if it is liquid and fit for drinking, its blessing is Shehakol, and if it is thick and fit for eating and chewing, its blessing is Mezonot. Its source is in the Rambam (Hilkhot Berakhot 3:3). Tosafot (Berakhot 38a) and Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona explained that a thick one is not called a beverage but a food, and the flour is primary; and a soft one that is fit for drinking is called a beverage, and the water in it is primary. What they wrote about food for 'chewing' does not mean specifically with one's teeth, but only to exclude a beverage that one drinks (Gra; MB 22; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 11). And Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 168:8 wrote: \"Fit for eating, that is … somewhat thick, such that people are not accustomed to gulp it down and swallow it all at once without turning it over with the tongue at all, which is the manner of drinking; rather, the manner is to turn it over a little with the tongue until it reaches the gullet, which is the manner of eating.\" In accurate editions of the Rambam the reading is 'to work it with the tongue' (lelo'ato), for this too is considered eating.<br>The same law applies to ice cream, cheese, leben and pudding, which are worked with the tongue. Some have doubts about this regarding dairy products and ice pops, either because they originate from liquids or because if they are left outside they will melt; but the view of most poskim is that they are treated as food (Ḥatam Sofer; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 202:9; Or Same'aḥ; Ben Ish Ḥai, Mas'ei 8; and many others), and this is how people speak. In addition, we have learned that some say the obligation of an after-blessing applies to liquids too with a kezayit and within akhilat peras, especially when this is the manner of drinking them (and so wrote in practice that they are treated as food: Sha'arei Ha-berakha 14:6; Birkat Hashem vol. 2, 1:16; Piskei Teshuvot 210:2; Ḥut Shani, Berakhot p. 211; and others)."},{"n":14,"en":"If one ate half a kezayit whose blessing is Me'ein Shalosh and half a kezayit whose blessing is Borei Nefashot, according to most poskim he recites the more basic blessing, which is Borei Nefashot (Knesset Ha-gedola; MA; MB 210:1; Ben Ish Ḥai, Mas'ei 5). Some say that the measures do not combine, and he does not recite a blessing (Kehunat Olam; Beit Meir). And in cases of doubt regarding blessings we are lenient (and so wrote Yabia Omer 8:23:25). But when it is a single food, the measures combine. However, if they are a complete mixture, like a pashtida, one goes according to the majority. And if they are attached as one food and not mixed, he recites Borei Nefashot (for example, cake with a filling above, halakha 9, and an 'American peanut' (a peanut coated in sweetened flour) below 11:5). (See the Harḥavot, and below 11:4, note 5.)"}]},{"slug":"10-10-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-10-12/","title_he":"יב – עד אימתי יכול לברך","title_en":"Until When One May Recite the Blessing","body_en":"<p>One must recite the after-blessing immediately upon finishing eating or drinking, and one should not occupy oneself with anything before the blessing. For it is not proper for a person to engage in his affairs before he thanks God for the food he ate, and furthermore, there is a concern that he may become absorbed in his affairs and forget to recite the blessing. In any case, one should hurry to recite the blessing as long as he is enjoying it and feels more satiated than he was before he ate. <i>Be-di'avad</i>, if he delayed and did not recite the blessing, and it is not clear to him that he now feels more satiated than he was before he began eating, if 72 minutes have not yet passed since he finished his eating or drinking, he may still recite the blessing. After that he should not recite it. And if it is clear to him that he still feels more satiated than he was before he ate, he may recite the blessing even after 72 minutes.<sup>15</sup></p><p>One who eats a satiating meal, even though he did not eat bread, <i>be-di'avad</i>, even if several hours have passed, as long as he feels a sensation of satiety he is obligated to recite the blessing. From the moment he feels a little hungry and begins to want to eat another meal, he has lost the blessing (see above 4:14, in the explanation of this measure).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":15,"en":"If he is more satiated than he was before he began, he can in any case recite the after-blessing, even if more than the time it takes to walk four mil, which is about 72 minutes, has passed (according to the view of R. Yoḥanan in Berakhot 53b). And if he is not more satiated than he was, according to most poskim, until the time it takes to walk four mil one may always recite the blessing, even if at that moment he is less satiated than he was before he began eating (for even Reish Lakish, who is stringent against R. Yoḥanan, agrees with this; Abudarham; Darkhei Moshe 184:1; Taz 184:2; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 8; BHL 184:5, s.v. \"im eino\"; Tzitz Eliezer 13:15; and see above 4:14). Some are concerned that if he is not more satiated than he was before he began eating, he should not recite the blessing (Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 184:3; and see MA 9). Sometimes this is difficult to estimate, for sometimes it is precisely the eating that arouses the appetite more. In any case, <i>le-khatḥila</i> one should take their view into account: if he does not know that he is more satiated, it is preferable that he eat more, and afterward recite the after-blessing (and regarding the first blessing, see above 9:5-7). And if he has no possibility of eating more, since the sensation of satiety is elusive and many poskim ruled that one may recite the blessing up to 72 minutes, even if he has become hungrier he should recite the blessing. (And if half an hour has not passed, there is no need to be stringent and eat more even if he has become hungrier, for he is still close to the eating; see the Harḥavot.)"}]},{"slug":"10-10-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-10-13/","title_he":"יג – הטועם ושותה בהפסקות אימתי יברך","title_en":"One Who Tastes and Drinks at Intervals: When Should He Recite the Blessing?","body_en":"<p>Sometimes a person intends to eat several fruits or several kinds of Mezonot over the next few hours, or to drink several cups, and the question arises: is it proper for him to recite one blessing at the beginning over everything he intends to eat and drink, and at the end an after-blessing over everything he ate and drank; or is it more proper for him to recite, over each act of eating and each cup, a blessing before it and a blessing after it? The rule is that if the acts of eating and drinking are considered one continuum, it is preferable to recite one blessing over everything. And if they are considered separate – for example, he pauses between them for a long time, or he eats them in different places – it is preferable to recite a blessing over each act of eating and drinking separately. Sometimes additional factors affect this, such as the placing of the foods [in front of him] or sitting with friends. Let us explain the halakha:</p><p>One who sits in one place and intends to pause between one act of eating and the next, and between one drink and the next, for less than half an hour, should properly combine all the acts of eating or drinking, and recite a first blessing over everything at the beginning, and an after-blessing over everything at the end. And if it is likely that he will pause for more than half an hour, since the pause is relatively long, it is preferable that he recite a first blessing and an after-blessing over each act of eating and each drink. In any case, <i>be-di'avad</i>, as long as he pauses less than 72 minutes between one act of eating and the next and between one drink and the next, he may still recite one blessing before all the acts of eating and drinking and one blessing after them.</p><p>If he placed in front of him a plate of fruit, a plate of cakes or a pitcher to drink from, since they are in front of him and he intends to continue eating or drinking from them, everything he eats and drinks from them is considered one continuum. Therefore, even if he sometimes pauses for more than half an hour, it is better that he recite one blessing over everything at the beginning, and one blessing after he finishes his eating or drinking. But if he knows that he will pause for more than 72 minutes, he should recite a first blessing and an after-blessing over each act of eating and each drink.</p><p>When a group has gathered at a party and has in front of them various refreshments and drinks, their sitting together, in addition to the refreshments before them, binds everything they eat and drink into one act of eating and drinking. They should recite blessings at the beginning over everything they will eat and drink, and after they finish eating and drinking they should recite an after-blessing. For example, if they had in front of them Mezonot, fruits and juice, they should recite at the beginning Mezonot, Ha-etz and Shehakol, and at the end Al Ha-miḥya and Borei Nefashot. Even if several hours pass between the first eating and the end of the party, as long as they are settled there and from time to time they taste the foods and drink the beverages in front of them, they have not lost the blessing over what they ate and drank at the beginning. Some are scrupulous to make sure that 72 minutes do not pass without their eating or drinking something. In any case, even one who paused for 72 minutes without eating or drinking has not lost the after-blessing, since he has not become hungry or thirsty in the meantime, for had he been hungry or thirsty, he presumably would have eaten and drunk more of what was in front of him.<sup>16</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":16,"en":"In this law there is a difference between the first blessing and the after-blessing. Regarding the first blessing, the Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Berakhot 4:7) that as long as he intends to continue eating, even if he paused for the entire day, he need not recite the blessing a second time. According to MA 184:9 and those who agree with him, if the time of digestion passed in the meantime, he has lost the first blessing. However, most poskim did not accept his view, as cited in MB 184:17, Tzitz Eliezer 12:1 and Yeḥaveh Da'at 6:11. But regarding the after-blessing, many Aḥaronim were concerned about the words of the MA, that if the time of digestion passed, he has lost the mitzva of reciting an after-blessing over what he ate and drank. And even if afterward he eats more food or drinks more beverage and recites an after-blessing over it, this blessing will no longer apply to what he ate and drank before the pause (BHL 190:2, s.v. \"aḥar\").<br>Since according to many the time of digestion for a small amount of eating is about 72 minutes (above note 15), one who intends to pause for 72 minutes between one act of eating and the next and between one drink and the next must recite a separate after-blessing for each act of eating and each drink. Consequently he will also recite a first blessing over each act of eating and each drink. But if the pitcher of drink is in front of him, or the plate of fruit or cakes is in front of him, and whenever he is a little hungry or a little thirsty he drinks and tastes from what is in front of him, there is no need for concern that the time of digestion has passed, for if he were a little hungry or thirsty he would have tasted and drunk from what was in front of him. Even so, <i>le-khatḥila</i> it is good that 72 minutes not pass between one act of eating and the next and between one drink and the next.<br>As for the question itself of what is preferable – to recite a blessing over each act of eating and each drink separately, or over everything together – there are many discussions (see Minḥat Yitzḥak 5:102; Tzitz Eliezer 12:1; Yabia Omer 6:27; Piskei Teshuvot 184:11). In my humble opinion, sometimes it seems that there is a dispute among the Aḥaronim, but in truth they were speaking of different situations. The rule that all agree to is that if all the acts of eating and drinking are considered in their eyes one continuum, they fulfill their obligation with one blessing over everything, and if they nevertheless split the acts of eating, they are considered as causing an unnecessary blessing. But if they regard each act of eating and drinking as standing on its own, they should recite a blessing over each act of eating and drinking (and see SA 169:3, regarding the law of the attendant). Based on this, if the pause is less than half an hour, there is almost no pause, for a period of half an hour is considered close and adjacent, and consequently everything is considered one continuum. More than half an hour – there is a pause, and each act of eating and drinking stands on its own. And if the food and the pitcher are in front of him, they connect the acts of eating and drinking. In any case, one who intends to wait more than 72 minutes – there is an interruption in this, and it is proper to recite a blessing over each act of eating and drinking separately, even though he has not become hungry or thirsty after the previous eating or drinking. Furthermore, there is a concern that perhaps he became a little hungry or thirsty and forgot to eat or drink, and since the time of digestion passed, he has lost the after-blessing over what he ate or drank at the beginning. And if they are a group, their group connects them (and so we learn from the Seder night; see Eshel Avraham of Butshash 474, who explains that even if the time of digestion passes, the group has the power to connect everything).<br>Regarding a party that lasts for hours, each act of eating creates continuity with the next; therefore, even if at the beginning he ate Mezonot and afterward fruits, and more than 72 minutes passed since he finished eating the Mezonot, as long as he has not become hungry, the acts of eating create continuity up to the after-blessing (based on MA 184:9 and Pri Megadim, Eshel Avraham 9; and this is the view of Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 473:10 and other Aḥaronim). Some wanted to infer from MB 184:18 that there is a distinction between a bread meal and eating other foods, in which eating the different foods does not create continuity (Shevet Ha-Levi vol. 7, 27). But it seems that the MB's intention is only where he did not settle himself for a party, but ate fruits and diverted his attention from continuing to eat fruits, and in the meantime ate Mezonot – in which case, according to the MA and those who agree with him, he has lost the after-blessing over the fruits. But when they have settled themselves for a party, their gathering combines all their eating into one act of eating, for as long as they have not withdrawn their hands from the foods and drinks in front of them, they may recite a blessing over everything they ate from beginning to end. One who wishes to be scrupulous should eat, within every period of less than 72 minutes, from each kind of food that establishes its own after-blessing. (There are four kinds of after-blessing: Me'ein Shalosh over Mezonot, over wine, over fruits, and Borei Nefashot.)"}]}]});
